+# Posting *The Ground* to the arXiv
+
+The paper is ready to post as a PDF submission; its content is complete at every revision, and every later revision replaces text in place. What follows is the metadata for the submission form, so that the posting is one act.
+
+**Title.** The Ground: Yang–Mills Theory after the Alpöge–Sussman Manuscripts. What Is Constructed, What Is Claimed, and the Object That Remains
+
+**Author.** Edwin Boston III
+
+**Primary category.** math-ph (Mathematical Physics). **Cross-lists.** hep-lat (High Energy Physics – Lattice), math.PR (Probability).
+
+**Comments field.** 31 pages. The generator of the document, the evidence register, the kernel record and the records of the computations are at https://github.com/edwinboston1-svg/III-Yang_Mills.
+
+**Abstract.** The abstract is the paragraph on the paper's title page (also the second paragraph of README.md); paste it as it stands.
+
+**File.** `THE-GROUND-Edwin-Boston-III.pdf` (the PDF is produced by Microsoft Word from the generator in `source/ground/`, not from TeX, and is submitted as a PDF).
+
+**Endorsement.** A first submission to math-ph may require an endorsement; the arXiv's form says so at submission and names the endorsers' requirement. The GitHub record carries the time stamps of every revision in the meantime.
+
+**What the paper claims, in one sentence for the submitter's own check.** A reflection-positive theory has a mass gap exactly when its reflection-positive correlations cluster exponentially (Theorem 7.1); a lattice gap uniform in physical units along a construction's sequence gives every limit point a unique vacuum, the cluster property and the gap (Theorem 7.4); and that uniform gap follows from two statements about one block at a fixed physical scale, a Dobrushin bound on the blocked measure's coarse variables and the clustering of the field inside the blocks, both uniform in the cutoff (Theorem 8.5). The paper proves these and names the two statements as the open mathematics; it does not claim the gap.
