+  push(P(`*The transform on a tube; Edwin Boston III.* For the SU(2) Wilson theory in 2 + 1 dimensions on the spatial torus of side two, in the temporal gauge, with the transfer matrix written in the spin-network basis truncated at spin *j* ≤ 1, a space of 2,018 states, and at *j* ≤ 3/2, a space of 33,342 states, Edwin Boston III reads the spectrum within the truncation and proves two facts about the operator. First, the inequality of Proposition 8.1 holds for its Doob chain, so that the gap is at least (1/*k*) log(1/δ(*P*^{k})) for every *k*. Second, the centre flux through the two cycles of the torus commutes with the transfer matrix, so the chain of Proposition 8.2 lumps exactly by the flux, with lumping defect zero: the physical rate is the gap within the sector of zero flux, and the full gap is the least of the gaps between sectors. A tube is not ℤ⁴, and the theorem supplies no uniformity in the cross-section; it is the first exact non-abelian instance of the structure the transform must carry. At *j* ≤ 1 and β₃ = 2.3 the top eigenvalue is 3.86580, the full gap 1.01886 is the torelon of unit flux, the zero-flux gap is 2.771287, and the plaquette correlation along the tube decays at the rates 2.849, 2.843 and 2.837 at separations 6, 7 and 8, falling toward the sector's gap; across β₃ from 0.2 to 6 the correlation decays at the sector's own rate, and at β₃ = 0.2 the rate 11.983 meets the strong-coupling value −4 log(*c*_{χ}/*c*₀) = 11.99 to 0.06 per cent. The truncation is not small, and every number is carried at its cutoff: read at *j* ≤ 3/2, the rate at β₃ = 2 falls by 6 per cent, and at β₃ = 2.3 the zero-flux gap reads 2.452280 against 2.771287, the torelon 0.931023 against 1.018863, and the correlation's rates 2.590, 2.563, 2.552 and 2.546 at separations 5 to 8 fall toward the sector's gap as before.[[fn:Edwin Boston III, the transform on a tube; the instrument, its records at both cutoffs and the run of record are listed in the Records. The spin-3/2 record at β₃ = 2.3 is posted with its digest in the repository; its gate run follows the run of record named there.]]`));
   push(P(`*The lattice theorems, checked by machine; Edwin Boston III.* In the Lean proof assistant, above the Mathlib library and with no unproved step, Edwin Boston III has formalised the following lattice theorems, and the Lean kernel has checked them.[[fn:Edwin Boston III, the lattice theorems in Lean; the files, the theorem names and the run of record are listed in the Records.]] For the transfer matrix of every finite-group lattice theory: every even-time quadratic form is non-negative; the spectral sum holds; the dominant root is simple and strictly dominant, with its residual law; the string energy is the exact limit of two Perron roots; and there is a mass gap on every finite cross-section, at every coupling. For every finite group acting faithfully by a real orthogonal representation, on every finite cross-section, the static potential of a Wilson loop is at least the mass gap of its sector. For the Z₂ theory: Griffiths' inequalities hold, with the monotonicity in the couplings; the infinite-volume Wilson expectation exists; the string tension exists, with its strong-coupling bound; and on a slab of *T* + 1 cross-sections of ℤ^{d−1} the mass gap is uniform in the cross-section and in *T* whenever (3*m* + 2) tanh β < 1, where *m* = 2(*d* − 2) is the number of in-plane plaquettes on a link, that is, for β < 0.1257 when *d* = 3 and β < 0.0716 when *d* = 4. Beside these stand the finite forms of the arguments of Sections 4 and 7: the bound that a gap imposes on every connected two-point function, the gap read off the decay, the definition of a physical gap along a scaling sequence, and Dobrushin's comparison theorem.`));
 
