   push(P(`Hypothesis I is read on the effective action of the coarse variables. Write the blocked measure as λ_{j}(*dv*) = *e*^{−A}*dv* with respect to the product over the blocks of a fixed reference measure on *V*, so that *A* = *A*_{j}(*v*) is the effective action at the coarse scale, and let Δ_{yy′}(*A*) be its mixed second difference in the sense of Proposition 8.4: the supremum, over coarse configurations differing only at *y*′, of the oscillation in *v*_{y} of the difference of the two actions. The conditional law of *v*_{y} under one of the two configurations is the tilt of its conditional law under the other by that difference, so the first inequality of Proposition 8.4 gives *C*^{λ}_{j}(*y*, *y*′) ≤ 1 − *e*^{−Δ} with Δ = Δ_{yy′}(*A*). Hypothesis I is therefore a bound on the mixed second differences of the effective action between blocks, weighted by their distance: the influence functional of §8.1, and the number that a certificate of the second link must deliver at the coarse scale.`, { before: 120 }));
   push(P(`One might wish to state the hypothesis on the fine measure directly, taking the blocks as the sites of a Gibbs measure and the whole configuration of a block as its variable, so that Dobrushin's condition for that measure would give every local observable's decay at once, with no fibre clause. That form is not uniform in the cutoff. Its interdependence coefficient between adjacent blocks is the total variation between two laws of all the links of a block under outside data that differ on a whole neighbouring face; as the spacing tends to zero that face carries ever more links, the two laws become distinguishable almost surely by an average over the face, and the coefficient tends to one at every coupling, so that the row sums tend to at least one. Whole-block total variation is the one norm that does not survive large blocks, and the hypothesis of the reduction is therefore stated on the coarse variables, whose type is fixed, with the field inside the blocks entering through its clustering alone.`, { before: 120 }));
   push(P(`Theorem 8.5 is the second link in the form the route attacks it. Hypothesis I is a bound on the blocked measure at the coarse scale, the object named in §8.1 as the influence functional of the blocked action. Hypothesis II is what the ultraviolet half must deliver below that scale; for the gauge group SU(2) the literature states nothing in this form, so it stands beside Hypothesis I as the second half of what the crossover must supply, and it is named as such. Proposition 8.4 is the fourth link in its exact form. The blocked measures λ_{j} have variables of one type at every rung, so it is meaningful to say that their conditional laws at the coarse scale are tilts of those of one rung *j*₁ by functions δ_{j} whose oscillations *o*_{j} and weighted mixed differences Σ_{y′}(1 − *e*^{−Δ})*e*^{κ|y−y′|} tend to zero; when they are, Hypothesis I at the rung *j*₁ with the constant α₁ implies it at every later rung with any α ∈ (α₁, 1), by (8.7). That the measures stand in this relation is the ultraviolet interface of §8.1, and it is the place at which Bałaban's theorem, with its two unpublished pieces, enters the chain.`));
-  push(P(`The theorem places the four-dimensional gap at one remove from a finite statement. Hypotheses I and II concern the conditional law of one coarse variable and the field inside one block, at a fixed physical scale, with constants uniform in the cutoff, and nothing in them refers to a limit; their consequence, by Theorem 8.5, is the unique vacuum, the cluster property and the mass gap of every limit point, with the gap's value in (8.8). The proof that follows this paper is the proof of those two statements, and its measure of progress is a number: the distance, in blocking steps, between the scale at which the ultraviolet half delivers its interface and the scale at which the blocked bound is read. On the hierarchical model of the renormalisation group, in which each blocking step is the Migdal–Kadanoff recursion on a single-plaquette weight and is exact for the hierarchical lattice, that distance has been measured: Edwin Boston III has certified the flow of the SU(2) Wilson weight at 256 bits of precision with directed rounding, every coefficient an interval and the truncation priced, and from β = 2.3 the fundamental character coefficient falls below the finite-group threshold 0.131 of §8.2 at the seventh step, from β = 5 at the eighteenth, and from β = 10 at the thirty-seventh.[[fn:Edwin Boston III, the certified descent; the instrument, its records at the four starting couplings and the run of record are listed in the Records.]] On that model the chain closes in a handful of steps from the crossover coupling; on ℤ⁴ those steps are the distance Hypotheses I and II must cross, and no reading of the hierarchical model is a statement about ℤ⁴. One more thing is known about the inequality that would carry the model's control to ℤ⁴. A comparison that bounded the mixed second differences of the effective action of ℤ⁴ by those of the hierarchical model, and held for every compact gauge group, would give the four-dimensional U(1) theory a gap wherever its hierarchical flow reaches the disk; the U(1) theory has a massless phase at weak coupling.[[fn:A. H. Guth, "Existence proof of a nonconfining phase in four-dimensional U(1) lattice gauge theory", *Phys. Rev. D* 21 (1980), 2291–2307; J. Fröhlich and T. Spencer, "Massless phases and symmetry restoration in abelian gauge theories and spin systems", *Comm. Math. Phys.* 83 (1982), 411–454.]] The inequality the step needs therefore depends on the group, and that dependence is the first property a proof of Hypothesis I must use.`));
+  push(P(`The theorem places the four-dimensional gap at one remove from a finite statement. Hypotheses I and II concern the conditional law of one coarse variable and the field inside one block, at a fixed physical scale, with constants uniform in the cutoff, and nothing in them refers to a limit; their consequence, by Theorem 8.5, is the unique vacuum, the cluster property and the mass gap of every limit point, with the gap's value in (8.8). The proof that follows this paper is the proof of those two statements, and its measure of progress is a number: the distance, in blocking steps, between the scale at which the ultraviolet half delivers its interface and the scale at which the blocked bound is read. On the hierarchical model of the renormalisation group, in which each blocking step is the Migdal–Kadanoff recursion on a single-plaquette weight and is exact for the hierarchical lattice, that distance has been measured: Edwin Boston III has certified the flow of the SU(2) Wilson weight at 256 bits of precision with directed rounding, every coefficient an interval and the truncation priced, and from β = 2.3 the fundamental character coefficient falls below the finite-group threshold 0.131 of §8.2 at the seventh step, from β = 5 at the eighteenth, and from β = 10 at the thirty-seventh.[[fn:Edwin Boston III, the certified descent; the instrument, its records at the four starting couplings and the run of record are listed in the Records. The first step from β = 2.2 is read from the same record: the coefficient rises from 0.464 to 0.486, an effective coupling of 2.34, where the one-loop running of ℤ⁴ takes 2.2 to 1.94 under one doubling of the scale.]] The record also says what the model is not. Its first step from β = 2.2 moves the coupling up where the running of ℤ⁴ moves it down, by an amount of the step's own size, so the model certifies the entry into the disk from its fifth rung and the threshold at its seventh, and not the crossover's first steps, which a flow on ℤ⁴ must read on an object of ℤ⁴; on ℤ⁴ those steps are the distance Hypotheses I and II must cross, and no reading of the hierarchical model is a statement about ℤ⁴. One more thing is known about the inequality that would carry the model's control to ℤ⁴. A comparison that bounded the mixed second differences of the effective action of ℤ⁴ by those of the hierarchical model, and held for every compact gauge group, would give the four-dimensional U(1) theory a gap wherever its hierarchical flow reaches the disk; the U(1) theory has a massless phase at weak coupling.[[fn:A. H. Guth, "Existence proof of a nonconfining phase in four-dimensional U(1) lattice gauge theory", *Phys. Rev. D* 21 (1980), 2291–2307; J. Fröhlich and T. Spencer, "Massless phases and symmetry restoration in abelian gauge theories and spin systems", *Comm. Math. Phys.* 83 (1982), 411–454.]] The inequality the step needs therefore depends on the group, and that dependence is the first property a proof of Hypothesis I must use.`));
   push(P(`Two things are known about the strong end of that distance. With the first-order transmission between links, the total variation moved by one neighbour's change, no block criterion of Dobrushin and Shlosman's kind gains on the single link at any block side, because a link deep inside a block branches to its eighteen neighbours exactly as a link of the full lattice does, and the criterion's series has the single link's ratio. A block gains only through the transmission across an integrated face, which carries the product of the two pinned means of the face's other edges, an average over the interior: its one-face constant is an explicit function of the moments of the von Mises–Fisher law on the three-sphere, the covariance of the transmitted variable being a multiple of the identity plus one rank-one term whose size is a closed form in two Bessel ratios. What is not in hand is the theorem that composes that transmission along paths inside a block: every composition that is proved, Dobrushin's comparison theorem among them, takes a supremum over configurations at each step and so composes the first-order sensitivities only, which return the single link's disk; a convergent expansion of the block measure would compose the averages, and its radius has no proof beyond that disk.[[fn:Edwin Boston III; the block criterion's accounting, the one-face law and the closed form of its covariance are entered on the ledger of the work that follows this paper, with their readings on the exact cube.]] The paper carries no number for that widening until both are theorems.`));
 
   push(H2('8.4  Coda'));
