@@ -104,7 +104,46 @@ module.exports = function (L) {
   push(P(`Theorem 8.5 is the second link in the form the route attacks it. Hypothesis I is a bound on the blocked measure at the coarse scale, the object named in §8.1 as the influence functional of the blocked action. Hypothesis II is what the ultraviolet half must deliver below that scale; for the gauge group SU(2) the literature states nothing in this form, so it stands beside Hypothesis I as the second half of what the crossover must supply, and it is named as such. Proposition 8.4 is the fourth link in its exact form. The blocked measures λ_{j} have variables of one type at every rung, so it is meaningful to say that their conditional laws at the coarse scale are tilts of those of one rung *j*₁ by functions δ_{j} whose oscillations *o*_{j} and weighted mixed differences Σ_{y′}(1 − *e*^{−Δ})*e*^{κ|y−y′|} tend to zero; when they are, Hypothesis I at the rung *j*₁ with the constant α₁ implies it at every later rung with any α ∈ (α₁, 1), by (8.7). That the measures stand in this relation is the ultraviolet interface of §8.1, and it is the place at which Bałaban's theorem, with its two unpublished pieces, enters the chain.`));
   push(P(`The theorem places the four-dimensional gap at one remove from a finite statement. Hypotheses I and II concern the conditional law of one coarse variable and the field inside one block, at a fixed physical scale, with constants uniform in the cutoff, and nothing in them refers to a limit; their consequence, by Theorem 8.5, is the unique vacuum, the cluster property and the mass gap of every limit point, with the gap's value in (8.8). The proof that follows this paper is the proof of those two statements, and its measure of progress is a number: the distance, in blocking steps, between the scale at which the ultraviolet half delivers its interface and the scale at which the blocked bound is read. On the hierarchical model of the renormalisation group, in which each blocking step is the Migdal–Kadanoff recursion on a single-plaquette weight and is exact for the hierarchical lattice, that distance has been measured: Edwin Boston III has certified the flow of the SU(2) Wilson weight at 256 bits of precision with directed rounding, every coefficient an interval and the truncation priced, and from β = 2.3 the fundamental character coefficient falls below the finite-group threshold 0.131 of §8.2 at the seventh step, from β = 5 at the eighteenth, and from β = 10 at the thirty-seventh.[[fn:Edwin Boston III, the certified descent; the instrument, its records at the four starting couplings and the run of record are listed in the Records. The first step from β = 2.2 is read from the same record: the coefficient rises from 0.464 to 0.486, an effective coupling of 2.34, where the one-loop running of ℤ⁴ takes 2.2 to 1.94 under one doubling of the scale.]] The record also says what the model is not. Its first step from β = 2.2 moves the coupling up where the running of ℤ⁴ moves it down, by an amount of the step's own size, so the model certifies the entry into the disk from its fifth rung and the threshold at its seventh, and not the crossover's first steps, which a flow on ℤ⁴ must read on an object of ℤ⁴. The distance Hypotheses I and II must cross is measured in the blocking steps of ℤ⁴ itself; no step count of the hierarchical model is a reading of it, and no reading of the hierarchical model is a statement about ℤ⁴. One more thing is known about the inequality that would carry the model's control to ℤ⁴. A comparison that bounded the mixed second differences of the effective action of ℤ⁴ by those of the hierarchical model, and held for every compact gauge group, would give the four-dimensional U(1) theory a gap wherever its hierarchical flow reaches the disk; the U(1) theory has a massless phase at weak coupling.[[fn:A. H. Guth, "Existence proof of a nonconfining phase in four-dimensional U(1) lattice gauge theory", *Phys. Rev. D* 21 (1980), 2291–2307; J. Fröhlich and T. Spencer, "Massless phases and symmetry restoration in abelian gauge theories and spin systems", *Comm. Math. Phys.* 83 (1982), 411–454.]] The inequality the step needs therefore depends on the group, and that dependence is the first property a proof of Hypothesis I must use.`));
   push(P(`Two things are known about the strong end of that distance. With the first-order transmission between links, the total variation moved by one neighbour's change, no block criterion of Dobrushin and Shlosman's kind gains on the single link at any block side, because a link deep inside a block branches to its eighteen neighbours exactly as a link of the full lattice does, and the criterion's series has the single link's ratio. A block gains only through the transmission across an integrated face, which carries the product of the two pinned means of the face's other edges, an average over the interior: its one-face constant is an explicit function of the moments of the von Mises–Fisher law on the three-sphere, the covariance of the transmitted variable being a multiple of the identity plus one rank-one term whose size is a closed form in two Bessel ratios. What is not in hand is the theorem that composes that transmission along paths inside a block: every composition that is proved, Dobrushin's comparison theorem among them, takes a supremum over configurations at each step and so composes the first-order sensitivities only, which return the single link's disk; a convergent expansion of the block measure would compose the averages, and its radius has no proof beyond that disk.[[fn:Edwin Boston III; the block criterion's accounting, the one-face law and the closed form of its covariance are entered on the ledger of the work that follows this paper, with their readings on the exact cube.]] The paper carries no number for that widening until both are theorems.`));
-  push(P(`One averaged composition is rigorous, and it is an instance of Theorem 8.5 itself. Choose a set of links of ℤ⁴ no two of which lie in one plaquette and which meets every plaquette; such a set exists, of density one quarter, with period four. Integrate those links and keep the others. Given the kept links the integrated ones are independent, each distributed by the law of its own six staples, so the field inside the blocks is a product measure and Hypothesis II holds exactly, with zero covariance and means that depend on the kept links of one plaquette; and the kept links form a Gibbs measure of range two whose interdependence matrix is computed from averages, since each plaquette carries exactly one integrated link and its weight, with that link integrated, is the ratio of two normalising constants of the von Mises–Fisher law. Dobrushin's theorem then applies to the kept links, and the row sum of their matrix is an explicit function of the coupling, through the mean and the covariance of that law: twelve direct terms carrying one pinned mean each and ninety mediated terms carrying the covariance, which is the Jacobian of the mean. Its root lies well above the single link's disk. Two lemmas make it a theorem and are named on the ledger: that the axial variance of the von Mises–Fisher law on the three-sphere is at most one quarter at every concentration, and that the third-order remainder of the logarithm of its normalising constant is bounded; when they are written, the number enters here. That no decimation of ℤ⁴ places both integrated links of every plaquette opposite one another, which a two-colouring argument shows for every dimension above two, is the reason the product of two pinned means cannot be composed this way, and the reason the wider widening remains the conjecture above.[[fn:Edwin Boston III; the perfect cover, the two-colouring argument, the range-two specification and its row sum are on the ledger with their records.]]`));
+  push(P(`One averaged composition is rigorous, and it is an instance of Theorem 8.5 in which the fibre clause holds exactly. Call a set of links of ℤ⁴ a *perfect cover* when every plaquette contains exactly one of its links. One exists with period two in every direction: reading the coordinates of a site *x* modulo 2, take the link from *x* in direction 1 when *x*₂ = *x*₃ = *x*₄, in direction 2 when *x*₄ = *x*₁ ≠ *x*₃, in direction 3 when *x*₂ = *x*₁ ≠ *x*₄, and in direction 4 when *x*₃ = *x*₁ ≠ *x*₂. The four conditions partition the sixteen residues, so the set has one link at every site and density one quarter, and a direct check of the ninety-six plaquettes of the torus of side two shows that each contains exactly one of its links; since the condition is local and the set periodic, the same holds on ℤ⁴ and on every torus of even side.[[fn:Edwin Boston III; the cover was found by an exhaustive search on the torus of side two and checked again, on a road sharing no line with the search, on every plaquette of the tori of side two, four and six, where a single bent site leaves ten plaquettes of the smallest torus with zero or two links of the set; Records R7.]] Two consequences are used below: no plaquette contains two links of the cover, and a link outside the cover lies in six plaquettes whose six cover links are distinct, since two links share at most one plaquette.`));
+  push(Thm('Lemma 8.6', 'The constants of the von Mises–Fisher law on the three-sphere', [
+    `For w in ℝ⁴ let ν_{w} be the law on the unit sphere S³ of ℝ⁴, identified with SU(2), whose density with respect to the uniform measure is proportional to e^{⟨u, w⟩}. Write r = |w|, Z(r) = 2I₁(r)/r for its normalising constant, φ = log Z and m = φ′ = I₂/I₁, with I_{ν} the modified Bessel functions. The mean of ν_{w} is m(r)ŵ; its covariance has the eigenvalue φ″(r) on ŵ and m(r)/r on the orthogonal complement; and its third cumulant along a unit vector u with ⟨u, ŵ⟩ = c is φ‴(r)c³ + 3(D(r)/r)c(1 − c²), where D = φ″ − m/r. For every r ≥ 0:`,
+    `(i) m(r)/r ≤ 1/4 and φ″(r) ≤ 1/4;`,
+    `(ii) −m(r)²/3 ≤ D(r) ≤ 0;`,
+    `(iii) the third cumulant of ν_{w} along every unit vector is at most m(r)/2 in absolute value.`,
+  ]));
+  push(Proof([
+    `The cumulants of ν_{w} are the derivatives at *w* of *x* ↦ φ(|*x*|) − φ(|*w*|), the logarithm of the normalised Laplace transform of ν_{w}. Its gradient at *w* is *m*ŵ and its Hessian has the stated eigenvalues; along the line *x* + *tu* the radius ρ(*t*) = |*x* + *tu*| has ρ′ = *c*, ρ″ = (1 − *c*²)/*r* and ρ‴ = −3*c*(1 − *c*²)/*r*² at *t* = 0, so the third derivative φ‴ρ′³ + 3φ″ρ′ρ″ + φ′ρ‴ is the stated form. The recurrences *I*₁′ = *I*₂ + *I*₁/*r* and *I*₂′ = *I*₁ − 2*I*₂/*r* give φ′ = *m* and the Riccati identity`,
+  ], 'Proof', true));
