   push(Proof(`Part (ii) is the content of the passages quoted in §§6.1–6.3: the conditional deductions and their clauses (§6.2), the two supporting statements not yet written (§6.2), the properties not claimed (§6.3), and the authors' description of the four-dimensional argument (§6.1). Part (iii) is the content of the disclaimer and of the README's sentences on verification quoted in §6.4.`, 'Proof of parts (ii) and (iii)'));
 
-  push(H2('6.6  Two further claims in public view'));
-  push(P0(`Two further claims stood in public view when this paper was written, and the ground is not fixed until each is placed beside the corpus. The first is an announcement. On 26 October 2026 OpenAI announced a mathematically rigorous existence proof of interacting quantum field theories in four dimensions, among them the Yang–Mills theory and quantum chromodynamics, stating that "We finished our formalization of Yang–Mills' existence on October 16th, 2026", and that a group led by David Gross, with Arthur Jaffe, Michael Douglas, Igor Klebanov and Nikita Nekrasov, would review the work before its release; Woit, who reproduces the announcement's sentences, gives its length as 1,147 pages, and the review group's own statement says that Bałaban's ideas "enabled OpenAI to follow the theory all the way to infinite distances and prove the existence of its proper continuum limit. But confinement and the mass gap remain very important open challenges."[[fn:Woit, *op. cit.*, the post of 26 October 2026, from which every sentence of the announcement and of the group's statement is quoted here; the work itself is unreleased, and this paper has read none of it.]] If that proof is released and holds, it is a candidate for the ultraviolet half of the chain of §8.1, with the effective measure at a fixed physical scale as its output; by its own statement it is not a proof of the gap.`));
+  push(H2('6.6  Further claims in public view'));
+  push(P0(`Four further claims stood in public view when this paper was written, and the ground is not fixed until each is placed beside the corpus. The first is an announcement. On 26 October 2026 OpenAI announced a mathematically rigorous existence proof of interacting quantum field theories in four dimensions, among them the Yang–Mills theory and quantum chromodynamics, stating that "We finished our formalization of Yang–Mills' existence on October 16th, 2026", and that a group led by David Gross, with Arthur Jaffe, Michael Douglas, Igor Klebanov and Nikita Nekrasov, would review the work before its release; Woit, who reproduces the announcement's sentences, gives its length as 1,147 pages, and the review group's own statement says that Bałaban's ideas "enabled OpenAI to follow the theory all the way to infinite distances and prove the existence of its proper continuum limit. But confinement and the mass gap remain very important open challenges."[[fn:Woit, *op. cit.*, the post of 26 October 2026, from which every sentence of the announcement and of the group's statement is quoted here; the work itself is unreleased, and this paper has read none of it.]] If that proof is released and holds, it is a candidate for the ultraviolet half of the chain of §8.1, with the effective measure at a fixed physical scale as its output; by its own statement it is not a proof of the gap.`));
   push(P(`The second is a published claim of the whole. Mir Faizal and Arshid Shabir, "Reflection-Positive Construction of a Four-Dimensional SU(N) Yang–Mills Theory with Mass Gap and Confinement", posted to the arXiv on 9 June 2026 and published in *Fortschritte der Physik* 74 (2026), e70097, with the detailed calculations in four companion papers in the *International Journal of Geometric Methods in Modern Physics*, runs to 593 pages in its arXiv form.[[fn:Faizal and Shabir, arXiv:2606.19362, doi 10.1002/prop.70097. This paper has read its abstract (p. 1), its table of contents (pp. 2–3) and the three statements at its join, Hypothesis 1 (p. 110), Theorem 10.4 (p. 116) and Theorem 10.8 (p. 128), and not its remaining pages; no expert assessment of it was found in print by 29 October 2026.]] Its abstract claims a reflection-positive lattice formulation with a transfer operator of uniform gap at strong coupling, a "finite-range, gauge-covariant multiscale analysis" that carries the gap and the area law "from one scale to the next with interlaced inequalities whose small defects can be summed", the Osterwalder–Schrader reconstruction, and universality: "a single-scale Lipschitz control and a telescoping argument bind all admissible reflection-positive slicings into a unique limiting measure", so that "the continuum reached from strong coupling meets the one approached along an asymptotically free trajectory, yielding one and the same theory". At the join the structure is this. Section 3 obtains the gap at strong coupling "at fixed lattice spacing" by the polymer expansion. Section 10 enters the weak-coupling regime under "Hypothesis 1 (Stated for convenience; proved as Theorem 10.4 below)", which asserts a one-step renormalisation map, analytic "in a neighborhood U of (g, K) = (0, 0)", obeying the one-loop recursion with a controlled remainder, Theorem 10.4 being the "one-step contraction near the Gaussian fixed point". Theorem 10.8, "Identification with the constructed continuum limit", then asserts that the continuum Schwinger functions "obtained by following an admissible, asymptotically free trajectory from the ultraviolet" and those "constructed by transporting the strong-coupling mass gap and Wilson-loop area law to the continuum within the admissible class" coincide, by the uniqueness of its Section 9 over that class. The passage between the two regimes is thus made by a uniqueness statement over a class of constructions, where the chain of §8.1 makes it by a bound at the crossover scale. Read at the five places where such a construction must carry weight, it carries it as follows.[[fn:The same arXiv PDF: §2.3 (p. 17), Proposition 5.5 (pp. 55–56), Theorem 5.6 (p. 57), Theorem 7.1 (p. 80), Theorem 9.4 (p. 105), the proof of Theorem 10.4 (pp. 117–119) and the appendix on the decomposition (p. 174). The quotations are verbatim.]] The multiscale analysis is a "scale-by-scale decomposition of Gaussian covariances", the resolvent of "a nonnegative, selfadjoint, gauge-covariant elliptic operator L on ℓ²(aℤ⁴) with nearest-neighbour structure and a spectral gap m² ≥ 0", with "massive covariances (A_{h} + μ²)^{−1}" in the appendix: the mass of the fluctuation field is a parameter of the decomposition, not an output of the theory. The persistence of the gap across scales, Theorem 5.6, assumes "the interlacing/defect inequality γ_{k+1} ≥ γ_{k} − δ_{k}" with Σδ_{k} < ∞ and concludes inf γ_{k} ≥ γ₀ − Σδ_{j} by the telescoping sum; the summability, Proposition 5.5, holds "Under the FRD locality hypotheses", which "guarantee", uniformly in the scale, that "every connected Ursell kernel U_{k}(X) associated with a finite polymer X of diameter diam(X) = ℓ obeys |U_{k}(X)| ≤ Ce^{−cℓ}" — exponential clustering at every scale is the hypothesis. The continuum Schwinger functions of Theorem 7.1 are subsequential limits, by tightness, of "the continuum embedding of the corresponding lattice field as a random tempered distribution … via block-averaging followed by the usual scaling", at a fixed bare coupling. The weak-coupling half, Theorem 10.4, is carried on three pages that represent "the one-step effective action by integrating the fluctuation field with law N(0, C₀)", a Gaussian, and state that "the Brydges–Kennedy–Abdesselam–Rivasseau (BKAR) formula together with Kotecký–Preiss bounds guarantees absolute convergence of the cumulant expansion provided the a priori polymer norm is small", with the remainder bounded because "Power counting and analyticity give the bound |R_{k}| ≤ c₂|g_{k}|⁵"; the large-field regions, the gauge fixing of the fluctuation field and the non-linear averaging of a compact variable, which occupy Bałaban's papers, do not appear in them. And the uniqueness that identifies the two limits, Theorem 9.4, concludes that two constructions give the same Schwinger functions "If μ_{k} = μ̃_{k} and K_{k} = K̃_{k} on a core of local observables", that is, when they already share their marginal and their transition kernel; so Theorem 10.8 presupposes the identification it asserts. In this form the construction does not reach the object this paper names as open, Hypothesis I of §8.3, and does not discharge it. Its remaining pages are not examined here, and no expert assessment of it was found in print by 29 October 2026.`));
+  push(P(`The third and fourth are manuscripts deposited on Zenodo, which no journal has refereed. Ramiro Fontes, "The SU(2) Yang–Mills Mass Gap via Curvature-Based Block Renormalization, Character Expansion, and Dobrushin Comparison", deposited on 7 March 2026 and revised on 11 March, states as its Main Theorem that for β ≥ β₀ = 9587 the connected correlators of gauge-invariant observables decay exponentially, uniformly in the volume, and that every subsequential continuum limit carries a mass gap (p. 4).[[fn:Zenodo records 18906249 and 18969699; this paper has read, in the version of 11 March 2026 (ym_v65.pdf), the Main Theorem (p. 4), the inductive hypothesis (p. 19), the passage on the decay rate (p. 46), the note on the terminal coupling (p. 80) and the clustering step of the mass-gap theorem (p. 85). B. Lee, Zenodo record 19601454, version v13_r22: pp. 11–12, 19, 26, 72 and 83.]] Its renormalisation group runs while the effective coupling stays at least β₀, the first of its inductive hypotheses (p. 19), and stops after (β − 9587)/0.258 steps; the decay is then asserted for the effective measure at β₀ by a cluster expansion whose polymers lie inside one block of side two with its boundary held fixed, the rate being, in its words, "the inter-block decay of the effective action" (pp. 46, 85). Locality of an effective action is not clustering of its measure, and the manuscript's own note shows the consequence, stating that at β = 9587, its minimum, "no RG step is needed: the cluster expansion converges directly on the original lattice" (p. 80). The bare Wilson theory at β = 9587, a coupling at which the manuscript's own normalisation places the physical scale some e^{25,800} lattice spacings out, would then have a correlation length of the order of the spacing. The mechanism is the spectral gap of the block's abelian Hessian, the non-abelian curvature entering as a small correction (p. 4), so the same argument would give the four-dimensional U(1) theory a gap at weak coupling, which Guth and Fröhlich and Spencer exclude: it fails the test of §8.3. Byoungwoo Lee, "Yang–Mills Mass Gap in Four Dimensions: Reduction and Closure for the BT–2 Route", deposited on 16 April 2026, obtains its gap m₀ ≥ ρ₀ from its Assumption 2.1, a logarithmic Sobolev floor uniform in the spacing (pp. 11–12). The floor is carried by the Holley–Stroock lemma under the supposition KL(μ₀‖μ) ≤ M (p. 72), with M among the quantities the manuscript holds fixed by choice (p. 11), and its appendix proves that the system of inequalities is solvable in its parameters (p. 83), not that the Yang–Mills measure satisfies it; for a Gibbs measure that relative entropy grows with the volume and as the spacing tends to zero. Neither manuscript reaches the crossover.`));
   return out;
 };
