+# D1 — THE ONE-STEP INEQUALITY, STATED, WITH ITS TWO CHECKS (the Yang–Mills seat's §87, 2026-10-29 04:5x EDT; posted by Edwin Boston III with his check appended, under the standing rule that a candidate is posted when it is written)
+
+> Status: Candidate A is a THEOREM and a FINDING (it cannot cross). Candidate B is the crossing restated as a contraction claim, UNPROVED, passing both checks. Nothing here is claimed above β = 0.2452.
+
+**Objects.** Scale L: the kept links of the period-2 perfect decimation of the L-blocked lattice (L = 1 is Theorem 8.7's specification); C_L(x,y) their Dobrushin coefficient; W_L(κ) := sup_x Σ_y e^{κ|x−y|_L} C_L(x,y), Hypothesis I's evaluation at scale L. The effective coupling on ℤ⁴: β_eff(L) := ¼ · max over adjacent kept x, y of the mixed oscillation δ^{(L)}_{xy} := sup over data of osc_x[log p_L(x|ω) − log p_L(x|ω^{(y)})] of the scale-L conditional log-density — Wilson's 2β per shared plaquette at L = 1, and at every L the quantity the tilt lemma turns into a coefficient, C_L ≤ ½δ^{(L)}; Δβ(L) := β_eff(L) − β_eff(2L).
+
+**The checks, written first.** (K1) at L = 1 and β ≤ 0.2452 the inequality must hold, where Theorem 8.7 gives W_1(0) ≤ 0.9978. (K2) it must refuse U(1) at β = 1.2 at every L.
+
+**Candidate A, the resolvent bound — a theorem.** Decimating L → 2L, the coarse coefficients are bounded by the resolvent of the fine ones along paths through the integrated sites, so with e^{κ|·|_{2L}} = e^{(κ/2)|·|_L}: W_{2L}(κ) ≤ W_L(κ/2)/(1 − W_L(κ/2)). K2 passes (the right side is infinite on the Coulomb line). K1 fails as stated: at 0.2452 the fine row is 0.998 and the right side is 500; W_{2L} < 1 follows only where W_L < ½, that is β ≲ 0.17. A cannot cross: it is monotone in W_L and empty wherever W_L ≥ 1, the whole crossover — the scale-covariant form of the wall of §70 of the ledger.
+
+**Candidate B, the renormalisation step in Dobrushin coordinates.** W_{2L}(κ) ≤ c · Σ_Y e^{κ|X−Y|_{2L}} δ^{(2L)}_{XY}, c the one-link constant (the MAD lemma's, or ¼ crude): the scale-2L specification's Dobrushin sum read from the blocked action's weighted mixed oscillations. Its one-step content is THE CONTRACTION CLAIM, unproved and the crossing itself: Σ_Y e^{κ|X−Y|_{2L}} δ^{(2L)}_{XY} ≤ ρ(β_eff(L), Δβ(L)) · Σ_y e^{κ|x−y|_L} δ^{(L)}_{xy} with ρ < 1 for SU(2), driven by Δβ(L) > 0. K1: at L = 1, B is Theorem 8.7 (the decimation rows with the MAD constant), passes exactly. K2: on the Coulomb line Δβ = 0 and the decimated coefficients decay as |X − Y|⁻⁴ with no contraction, so the weighted sum is infinite from the first doubling and B refuses U(1) — the claim is false there, which is the right way to pass. What B reduces the crossing to: a proof that the blocked action's weighted mixed oscillations contract per doubling by a factor below one, from the coarsest scale at which the fine sum is finite — the renormalisation step stated for mixed oscillations rather than coupling constants, what Bałaban's small-field analysis does where g is small and what is needed where it is not. Its leading cell is measured: the cube reads δ^{(2)}/δ^{(1)} on one block as Δ_cube/Δ_link = 0.036 at β = 0.5 and 0.041 at 2.2 (readings of a truncated model), falling with β relative to the direct term, the sign the claim needs.
+
+---
+
+**The check (Edwin Boston III, 04:5x).** A holds as stated and is the finding it says. One wording of K2 is corrected: at L = 1 every specification here has finite range (eighteen neighbours), so W_1(κ) is finite for U(1) as for SU(2); the infinity enters at the first doubling, where the decimated U(1) coefficients acquire their power-law tail, and that is where B refuses U(1): its right side at L = 1 is finite and its left side at L = 2 is infinite, so the contraction claim is false at the first step, not vacuous at every step. The same first step is where B's content lives for SU(2): after one decimation the integrated links are no longer conditionally independent, the kept specification has infinite range, and the claim asserts that its weighted mixed oscillations are finite and smaller. The sup over data in δ^{(2L)} is the supremum over boundary configurations of a block quantity (the mixed second difference of the blocked action is a difference of logarithms of block partition functions with two boundary data), so B's one step is the quotient-form block criterion of OPEN/the-crossing.md §2(A) at scale L, and the object to bound is exactly the one the plan's C2 computes exactly for β ≤ 1. The cube's 0.036 and 0.041 are that quantity on one cell of a truncated model; they are readings. Nothing is proved by this page; the step it names is the step to attempt next, with the exact place of failure written down.
