ange whose Dobrushin row sum is at most 0.9963, with constants proved from Turán's inequality for the modified Bessel functions (Lemma 8.6); the infinite-volume Gibbs measure is therefore unique, correlations of local observables decay exponentially, and the transfer matrix has a mass gap. The numbers of its certificate are evaluated on three roads that share no line, and with the exact constants, enclosed in a gate run of record, the bound extends to β ≤ 0.2452; the derivation's test from outside, a search from below over the decimated specification, found no configuration exceeding its constants, and a second reader's independent line-by-line read found no defect. The theorem is a statement about the lattice theory at strong coupling; it reaches no crossover and proves nothing about the continuum limit, where the four-dimensional mass gap remains open. The paper closes Section 8.3 by stating the open part exactly: the group enters only through the evaluation of Hypothesis I, the weak end has no published coupling (Bałaban's threshold is an existential constant on every effective coupling of the sequence, read from his pages), the distance is [0.2452, 1/γ²] with γ unread, and the tools that would cross it are named and not claimed. Section 6.6 places four further claims in public view beside the corpus: the OpenAI announcement of 26 October 2026, the published paper of Faizal and Shabir, read at the five places where its argument must carry weight, and the Zenodo manuscripts of Fontes and of Lee, each read at the page where its gap is obtained; Section 8 carries, of record, the sharp one-link constant 2β/(3π) with the disks 3π/72 and 3π/56, the certified hierarchical descent and the transform on an exact tube, and states why the block criterion's widening is not yet a theorem.
 
